Moral responsibility requires that the agent could have done otherwise — and compatibilist free will preserves this: an agent acts freely when no external compulsion prevents them from acting on their own reasoning and values.
Every functioning legal system treats humans as moral agents whose deliberation can be influenced by incentives, laws, and argument — and the practical success of these systems shows this model works.
Holding people responsible — praising, blaming, punishing, rewarding — only makes sense if their actions flow from their character, values, and reasoning: which is precisely what the compatibilist account of free will provides.
Without moral responsibility, the concepts of justice, desert, guilt, and achievement collapse — yet these concepts track something real in human experience that cannot simply be explained away.
